===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*weǵ-}}
A merger of two verbs of similar form and meaning:
* {{inh|en|enm|waken}}, {{inh|en|ang|wacan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wakan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wakaną}}.
* {{inh|en|enm|wakien}}, {{inh|en|ang|wacian}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wakēn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wakāną}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|wakes|waking|woke|past2=waked|woken|past_ptc2=waked|past_ptc3=woke|past_ptc3_qual=now colloquial}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} (often followed by ''[[up]]'') To stop [[sleep]]ing.
#: {{ux|en|I '''woke''' up at four o'clock this morning.}}
#* {{RQ:Falkner Moonfleet|year=1934|passage=How long I slept I cannot tell, for I had nothing to guide me to the time, but '''woke''' at length, and found myself still in darkness.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} (often followed by ''[[up]]'') To make somebody stop sleeping; to [[rouse]] from sleep.
#: {{ux|en|The neighbour's car alarm '''woke''' me from a strange dream.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Zechariah|4|1||And the Angell that talked with me, came againe and '''waked''' me, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}} To put in motion or action; to arouse; to excite.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=Samson Agonistes|59|passage=Not for my life, leſt fierce remembrance '''wake''' <br> My ſudden rage to tear thee joint by joint.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1880|author=w:John Richard Green|title=History of the English People
|passage=Even Richard's crusade '''woke''' little interest in his island realm.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} To be excited or roused up; to be stirred up from a dormant, torpid, or inactive state; to be active.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=IX|lines=93–94|passage=and gentle Aires due at thir hour <br> To fan the Earth now '''wak'd''',|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Keble Christian Year|volume=I|poem=Easter Day|page=146|passage=Then '''wake''', my soul, to high desires, <br> And earlier light thine altar fires: {{...}} }}
# To watch, or sit up with, at night, as a dead body.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1824|author=Sir Walter Scott|title=Redgauntlet|passage=Dougal said that being alone with the dead on that floor of the tower (for naebody cared to '''wake''' Sir Robert Redgauntlet like another corpse) he had never daured{{sic}} to answer the call, but that now his conscience checked him for neglecting his duty; {{...}} }}
# To be or remain awake; not to sleep.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Ecclesiasticus|42|9||The father '''waketh''' for the daughter when no man knoweth, and the care for her taketh away sleepe;}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=3|lines=696–697|passage=And oft though wiſdom '''wake''', ſuspicion ſleeps <br> At wiſdoms Gate,|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding}}, ''Book II, Chapter I''
#*: I cannot think any time, '''waking''' or sleeping, without being sensible of it.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To be alert; to keep watch
#: {{ux|en|Command unto the guards that they diligently '''wake'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To sit up late for festive purposes; to hold a night revel.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|act=I|scene=iv|passage=The king doth '''wake''' to-night, and takes his rouse, <br> Keeps wassail, and the swaggering upspring reels.}}

