===Etymology 3===
Probably from {{bor|en|gml|-}} or {{der|en|dum|wake}}, from or akin to {{der|en|non|vǫk|t=a hole in the ice}} ( > {{cog|da|våge}}, {{cog|is|vök}}), from {{der|en|gem-pro|*wakwō|t=wetness}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wegʷ-|t=moist, wet}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[path]] left [[behind]] a [[ship]] on the [[surface]] of the [[water]].
# The movement of water created when an animal or a person moves through water.
# {{lb|en|aviation}} The [[turbulent]] [[air]] left [[behind]] a [[fly]]ing [[aircraft]].
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} The [[area]] [[behind]] something, typically a [[rapid]]ly-[[move|moving]] [[object]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1826|author=w:Thomas De Quincey|title=Lessing|journal=Blackwood's Magazine
|passage=This effect followed immediately in the '''wake''' of his earliest exertions.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Virginians|passage=Several humbler persons {{...}} formed quite a procession in the dusty '''wake''' of his chariot wheels.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|chapter=5|title=w:Merchants of Doubt|author=w:Naomi Oreskes; w:Erik M. Conway|passage=It was all of a piece. If you believed in capitalism, you had to attack science, because science had revealed the hazards that capitalism had brought in its '''wake'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=September 28, 2011|author=Tom Rostance| title=Arsenal 2 - 1 Olympiakos| work=BBC Sport| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14998317.stm| passage={{w|Alex Song}} launched a long ball forward from the back and the winger took it down nicely on his chest. He cut across the penalty area from the right and after one of the three defenders in his '''wake''' failed to make a meaningful clearance, the {{w|Alex Oxlade-Chamberlain|Oxlade-Chamberlain}} was able to dispatch a low left-footed finish into the far corner.}}

